  "notes": "Frank Lloyd Wright's rotunda (1959) on Fifth Avenue between 88th and 89th Streets, now set on Manhattan's grid, which runs 29 degrees east of true north (read off the streets in the OpenStreetMap ground; the rectilinear parts are rotated boxes drawn as meshes, the round ones need no turning): the inverted spiral of white bands widening upward with dark slots between, over a glazed ground floor, the ribbed glass skylight on top (about 60% of the roof's width, inside a white ring), at the 88th Street end; the Monitor (the small rotunda, 48 ft across and 48 ft high) at the 89th Street end; the long low base along the avenue between them, glazed at street level; Gwathmey Siegel's 1992 limestone tower rising behind the Monitor with its bands of windows; the sidewalk, Fifth Avenue, the park wall and Central Park's trees across the avenue; 88th and 89th Streets. Refined 24 Sep 2026 without photographs, from the museum's and Google Arts & Culture's published descriptions (rotunda about 96 ft to the oculus; small rotunda half the main one's size) and the ground file's street lines: the first model stood square to the page with the avenue running true north-south; it is now turned to the real grid and placed on its lot (the geocoded point lies near the lot's north end, so the rotunda sits south-west of the site's centre), the Monitor made smaller. Guessed: the band radii (about 11 m at the bottom, 15.5 m at the top), the Monitor's shape (a drum; it is a rounded oblong), the tower's footprint and grid, the lot depth. No photographs were available. Second pass: the skylight shrunk from nearly the full roof to about 19 m across inside the white roof ring; Central Park thickened with trees. Third pass: the street trees in front of the rotunda removed, so the rotunda stands clear to the avenue as it is always seen; one kept toward 89th Street."
}
